    "answer": {
      "short": "$3\\sin t+4\\cos t=5\\sin\\left(t+\\tan^{-1}\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$. Maximum depth: $10$ m, first occurring at approximately $12{:}39$ am.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}R\\sin(t+\\alpha)&=R\\cos\\alpha\\sin t+R\\sin\\alpha\\cos t.\\end{aligned}$$\nComparing coefficients:\n$$\\begin{aligned}R\\cos\\alpha&=3\\qquad(1)\\\\R\\sin\\alpha&=4\\qquad(2).\\end{aligned}$$\nSquaring and adding (1) and (2):\n$$\\begin{aligned}R^2\\cos^2\\alpha+R^2\\sin^2\\alpha&=9+16\\\\R^2&=25\\\\R&=5\\qquad(R>0).\\end{aligned}$$\nDividing (2) by (1), with $\\alpha$ in quadrant I:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac{4}{3}\\\\\\alpha&=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}$$\nTherefore\n$$\\begin{aligned}3\\sin t+4\\cos t&=5\\sin\\left(t+\\tan^{-1}\\frac{4}{3}\\right),\\\\d&=5+5\\sin\\left(t+\\tan^{-1}\\frac{4}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}$$\nThe maximum occurs when the sine is $1$, so the maximum depth is $5+5=10$ metres.\nFor the first maximum after midnight, using radians:\n$$\\begin{aligned}t+\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\t&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\\\t&\\approx0.6435\\text{ hours}\\\\t&\\approx38.6\\text{ minutes}.\\end{aligned}$$\nThe maximum first occurs at approximately $12{:}39$ am, to the nearest minute.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
